From: SuperLeiyan <SuperLeiyan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 54/54] PC5: a copy of the app under another title id finds its
 own sandbox

The title id was the one the eboot was built with (PW_TITLE_ID), and the
sandbox view of app0 that holds the Wine runtime once /data is granted was
looked for under it: /mnt/sandbox/<PW_TITLE_ID>_00N/app0. A copy of the app
registered under another id, as a PC5 home screen tile is, runs in
/mnt/sandbox/<its id>_00N instead, so it found no runtime ("pc5 files
missing from the app") and fell back to the plain launcher. On a console a
copy of PC5 as PPSA99437, with a Lapy helper made for that id, did just
that: /data was granted, the runtime was not found.

The title now reads its id first, while /app0 is still visible: the titleId
of /app0/sce_sys/param.json (pw_title_id_read), the id the console
registered the folder with. The sandbox path, the search for the sandbox
instance, the restart through the sandbox eboot and the log identity use
it. When param.json cannot be read the built-in id is used, as before. The
log names it: "PW_WINE64 title id=<id> from=param.json|built-in".

The reader is a header (native/pw_title_id.h) included by wine64_main.c and
its host test, so the eboot's source list in tools/build_native.sh is left
as it is.
